TITLE: QED at Finite Temperature
TAGLINE: Euclidean time is periodic with period 1/T, Matsubara frequencies replace k⁰, and the partition function is a path integral on a circle
NUM: 84
NEXT: Lesson 85 puts QED in a strong external field, pair production without a second real photon, the process Lesson 57 postponed.

# How to use this lesson

Wick rotation (Lesson 66) turns e^{iS} into e^{−S_E}. At temperature T the Euclidean time is compact with period β=1/T: bosons periodic, fermions antiperiodic. Loop integrals become sums over Matsubara frequencies. This lesson derives those boundary conditions from Tr e^{−H/T} and stops short of a full thermal rate. Expect five to seven hours.

## Learning objectives

- Identify the thermal partition function with a Euclidean path integral of period 1/T.

- Derive bosonic periodicity φ(τ+β)=φ(τ) from the trace.

- Derive fermionic antiperiodicity ψ(τ+β)=−ψ(τ) from the Grassmann trace.

- Replace ∫dk⁰/2π by T Σ_n, with ω_n=2πnT (bosons) or 2π(n+1/2)T (fermions).

