### Worked example 1

Why is T→0 ordinary QED? β→∞ decompactifies Euclidean time, recovering Lesson 66's Wick-rotated vacuum theory. Thermal corrections are e^{−m/T} or powers of T/m, which vanish in that limit.

# 2 Bosons versus fermions

A trace over bosonic states identifies φ(β) with φ(0): periodic. A trace over Grassmann states, Tr F=Σ ⟨n|F|n⟩, introduces a minus when a fermion is cycled past the other fermions in the complete-set insertion, which is equivalent to antiperiodic boundary conditions ψ(β)=−ψ(0). Photons are bosons. Electrons are fermions.

The Fourier modes on the circle are then e^{iω_n τ} with

$$ ω_n = 2π n T          (photons, n ∈ ℤ)

$$ ω_n = 2π (n + 1/2) T  (electrons, n ∈ ℤ)

A loop integral ∫d⁴k/(2π)⁴ becomes T Σ_n ∫d³k/(2π)³, with k⁰ replaced by iω_n.

# 3 What thermal QED changes, and what it does not

The Feynman rules of Lesson 49 still apply, with that replacement. The Ward identity still kills the photon mass: a thermal plasma does not give the photon a Lorentz-invariant mass, though it does generate a Debye screening length for the electric field. Infrared divergences of Lesson 73 are worse, because a Bose factor 1/(e^{ω/T}−1)∼T/ω is singular at ω=0. This lesson does not compute a thermal production rate; it warns that the vacuum Bloch–Nordsieck cancellation is not the last word in a plasma.

### Worked example 2

A photon at rest in the plasma has ω=0 in the vacuum sense. Is that mode thermal? The n=0 Matsubara photon is the static mode, responsible for Debye screening. It is not a propagating on-shell photon of Lesson 37. Confusing the two is how one might think the photon had acquired a mass in conflict with Lesson 71.

